  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Texas received $77.3B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 158,566 awards with an average award size of $487K. Obligations fell 10.5% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations show variability: FY21 was $55.1B, FY22 was $67.6B, FY23 was $81.9B, FY24 was $59.3B, FY25 was $86.3B, and FY26 is $77.3B. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry by obligations at $17.5B, followed by Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing at $11B and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $10B. The Department of Defense and the Department of Homeland Security are the largest buying agencies, obligating $31.3B and $24.9B respectively, with the Department of Veterans Affairs obligating $16B. McKesson Corporation and Lockheed Martin Corporation lead vendors with $10.9B and $10.5B respectively, followed by a second Lockheed Martin Corporation entry at $7.3B. The data are contract obligations and reflect fiscal year to date totals.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did federal contracting in Texas compare across fiscal years?",
        "body": "FY25 had the largest obligations at $86.3B, and FY21 had the smallest in the listed span at $55.1B. FY23 obligations reached $81.9B, FY22 reached $67.6B, FY24 reached $59.3B, and FY26 to date is $77.3B. The FY2026 to date total is down 10.5% year over year."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate federal contracting in Texas?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense leads buyers with $31.3B, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $24.9B and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $16B. MCKESSON CORPORATION is the top vendor with $10.9B, LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION follows with $10.5B and a separate entry at $7.3B, and FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO is next at $4.6B."
      }
    ]
  },
